WEBVTT

NOTE Reviewed speech captions.

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.580
Welcome back, everyone. Today we're going to open up a neural network and

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:09.260
watch it learn step by step. We'll start with the forward pass, where

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.060
inputs are multiplied by weights and pass through an activation function,

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.380
like ReLU or sigmoid. Then we'll measure the loss, which tells us how wrong

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.120
the prediction was. After that comes backpropagation, where we use the

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.500
chain rule to work out how much each weight contributed to the error.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.020
Finally, we update the weights using gradient descent.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.820
Repeat that thousands of times, and the network learns.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.060
It's simpler than it sounds, so let's get into it.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.480
Here's our network before any training.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.840
Its job is to predict which of these 40 students will pass an exam.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:47.940
Right now, it's guessing.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.860
It gets about half of them right, which is no better than a coin toss.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.500
Now, watch what 150 tiny corrections can do.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:06.240
93%. Nobody told it the rule. It found the rule on its own. So how did it do that?

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.760
Let's rewind, slow down and follow one student, one number at a time.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.120
Let's meet

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.880
the network properly. It takes two numbers about a student and predicts

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.920
whether they'll pass. Two inputs go in, hours studied and hours slept, both scaled

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.520
to sit between zero and one. A hidden layer of two neurons uses

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.760
ReLU, the rectified linear unit.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.860
The single output neuron uses a sigmoid

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.100
to produce a probability.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.740
Every connection carries a weight.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.500
Blue is positive, orange is negative,

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:40.880
and thicker means stronger.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.440
Let's zoom into the first hidden neuron, H1.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.800
Every neuron does two things, a weighted sum,

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:49.600
then an activation.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:52.720
Each input travels along its connection

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.740
and gets multiplied by that connection's weight.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:01.720
So, 1 times 0 .5 is 0 .5, and 0 .5

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:08.020
times minus 0 .3 is minus 0 .15. Then we add the neuron's

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:14.020
bias, 0 .1. The result is called Z, the linear combination. For neuron

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:16.960
H1, Z1 equals 0 .45.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.720
Next, Z passes through an activation function. Our hidden layer uses ReLU.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:25.680
ReLU keeps positive

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.860
values and turns negative values into zero. In symbols, it equals the

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:32.320
maximum of zero and z.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:38.900
Z1 is 0 .45. That's positive, so neuron H1 outputs

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.740
a 1 equals 0 .45.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:47.720
Neuron H2 does the same. Z2 is 0 .5, so a 2 is 0 .5.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.460
Why bother? Without activations,

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.780
stacking layers would collapse into one straight line model.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.960
The output neuron repeats the same pattern,

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:00.240
using the hidden outputs as its inputs.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:07.780
0 .45 times 0 .7, plus 0 .5 times minus 0 .4,

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.600
plus the bias of 0 .2, gives z equals 0 .315.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.120
The output uses sigmoid, which squashes any number

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.380
into a probability between 0 and 1.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:24.340
Sigmoid of 0 .315 is 0 .578.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:29.620
The network says, a 57 .8 % chance that this student passes.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.620
That whole journey from inputs to prediction is called forward propagation.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:40.340
This student actually passed, so the true label Y equals 1.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.100
To measure how wrong the prediction is, we use binary cross -entropy loss.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.700
When Y equals 1, the loss becomes minus the log of Y hat,

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.620
being confident and right costs very little,

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.300
being confident and wrong costs a lot.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:04:00.520
Minus the log of 0 .578 is 0 .548.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.100
The whole goal of training is to push this number down.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.540
In practice, frameworks like PyTorch fuse the sigmoid

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.640
and the cross entropy into one function,

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.260
BCEWithLogitsLoss, for numerical stability.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.740
Now we work backwards to find how much each weight

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:19.980
is to blame for the error.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:25.740
This is backpropagation. Start at the output. For a sigmoid with cross entropy,

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:32.120
the gradient simplifies beautifully. It's just y hat minus y, 0 .578

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:33.620
minus 1, which

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:39.020
is minus 0 .422. Each output weights gradient is that error,

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:40.880
times the activation flowing

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.780
into it. Minus 0 .422 times 0 .45 gives

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:51.440
minus 0 .19. To go further back, we use the chain rule.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:56.300
Multiply by the connecting weight, then by ReLU’s slope, which is one here,

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:57.760
because z is positive.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:03.480
Then each input weights gradient is that error times its input. Now every

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:04.740
weight knows which way to move.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:11.540
Now the update, gradient descent nudges every weight a small step against its gradient.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.660
The step size is the learning rate, eta.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:20.680
With eta equal to 0 .5, the weight V1 becomes 0 .7 minus

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.880
0 .5 times minus 0 .19.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:29.380
That's 0 .795, a negative gradient pushes a weight up.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:31.820
Same student, forward again.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:37.280
The prediction rises to 0 .719, and the loss falls to 0

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.340
.329, one step, and the network has already improved.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:47.660
That was one update from one student. Now let's go back to all 40 students.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.420
The white line is the decision boundary. On one side, the network predicts

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.480
pass. On the other side fail.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:06:01.200
Now we repeat the same four steps over and over, forward pass, loss, back propagation update.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.460
With every update, the loss falls and the boundary shifts to separate the two groups.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:12.960
After 150 updates, we're back at 93%, the result you saw at the start.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.880
And now, you know exactly how it got there.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.420
Let's recap the whole process.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:26.620
Forward pass, each neuron computes a weighted sum plus a bias, then applies an activation.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.440
Loss, compare the prediction with the truth to measure how wrong it was.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:36.140
Backpropagation, use the chain rule to find how much each weight

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:37.260
contributed to the error.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:42.080
Update, nudge every weight against its gradient, scaled by the learning rate.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:48.320
Our little network had just nine numbers to learn, six weights and three biases.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:55.660
Today's largest models have billions of them, yet they learn with these same four steps,

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:57.200
repeated millions of times.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.200
Now it's your turn.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:05.500
Open the back prop playground, change the learning rate, the optimizer and the network shape,

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:06.700
and watch what happens.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:08.720
See you in the next class.
